      "WRW",
      "[Expert R1] (Detect atomicity violations in the code.)\n```json\n{\n    \"violations\": [\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"i\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Write\", \"Read\", \"Write\"],\n            \"lines\": [14, 20, 14]\n        }\n    ]\n}\n```\n\n[System] Expert first round completed in 3.24 seconds\n\n[Judge R1] (Validate the expert's defect report and check control/interrupt conditions.)\nLet me analyze each read/write operation for the global variable `i` to determine if the reported atomicity violation can actually occur.\n\n## Step 1: Check Control Conditions\n\n### Operations in svp_simple_329_001_isr_1:\n- **Line 14**: `i = j + 1;` (Write operation)\n  - Control condition: `for (int k = 0; k < NUM; k++)`\n  - Variable `k` is initialized to 0 and incremented by 1 each iteration\n  - `NUM` is defined as 10 (constant)\n  - This loop will execute 10 times (k=0,1,2,...,9), so the write operation at line 14 will be executed 10 times\n\n### Operations in svp_simple_329_001_isr_2:\n- **Line 20**: `j = i + 1;` (Read operation of `i`)\n  - Control condition: `for (int k = 0; k < NUM; k++)`\n  - Similar to above, this loop will execute 10 times\n  - The read operation of `i` at line 20 will be executed 10 times\n\n## Step 2: Check Interrupt Status\n\nThe code does not contain any `disable_isr()` or `enable_isr()` calls. This means:\n- Both ISRs (svp_simple_329_001_isr_1 and svp_simple_329_001_isr_2) can preempt each other based on their priority levels\n- ISR 1 has priority 1, ISR 2 has priority 2 (lower priority)\n- Both ISRs can also preempt the main function\n\n## Step 3: Execution Analysis\n\nGiven that:\n- Both ISRs can execute (no interrupt disabling)\n- Both loops will execute their iterations\n- The read/write operations will actually occur\n\nThe reported atomicity violation pattern [\"Write\", \"Read\", \"Write\"] with lines [14, 20, 14] represents:\n1. Write to `i` at line 14 in ISR 1\n2. Read from `i` at line 20 in ISR 2\n3. Write to `i` at line 14 in ISR 1 (again)\n\nThis pattern can occur because:\n- ISR 1 can write to `i` (line 14)\n- ISR 2 can then read from `i` (line 20) \n- ISR 1 can write to `i` again (line 14) due to preemption\n\n## Summary of Analysis\n\nAll read/write operations mentioned in the defect report can be executed:\n- Line 14 (write to `i`) will execute 10 times in ISR 1\n- Line 20 (read from `i`) will execute 10 times in ISR 2\n- No interrupt disabling prevents these operations\n- The control conditions for both loops will be satisfied\n\nThe atomicity violation remains valid.\n\n```json\n{\n    \"violations\": [\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"i\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Write\", \"Read\", \"Write\"],\n            \"lines\": [14, 20, 14]\n        }\n    ]\n}\n```\n\n[Expert R2] (Refine the report based on judge feedback, re-check for missed or false defects.)\n```json\n{\n    \"violations\": [\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"i\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Write\", \"Read\", \"Write\"],\n            \"lines\": [14, 20, 14]\n        },\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"j\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Write\", \"Read\", \"Write\"],\n            \"lines\": [20, 14, 20]\n        }\n    ]\n}\n```\n\n"
